course: religion-ethics
lesson: 6
title: Moses and the Exodus
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: In Egypt the Hebrews were slaves, and Pharaoh ordered their baby boys to be killed; two brave midwives refused, and Moses' mother hid her baby in a basket on the River Nile, where Pharaoh's own daughter found him and raised him.
explain: Years later God spoke to Moses from a bush that burned but did not burn up, gave his name «I AM WHO I AM», and sent him to tell Pharaoh: «Let my people go».
explain: Pharaoh refused again and again, ten plagues struck Egypt, and on the night of the Passover the Israelites left; God opened a way through the Red Sea and they walked out free.
explain: In the desert God fed them with manna, enough for each day, but the journey to the Promised Land took forty years, and the people often grumbled.
explain: Christians believe this story shows that God sees people who are treated unfairly and sets them free; for a child today it means standing up for the weak, being brave like the midwives, and trusting God one day at a time.
fact: Pharaoh ordered Hebrew baby boys to be killed, and Moses' mother hid him in a basket on the Nile.
fact: Pharaoh's daughter found the baby Moses and raised him as her own son.
fact: Two Hebrew midwives, Shiphrah and Puah, refused Pharaoh's order to kill the baby boys.
fact: God spoke to Moses from a bush that was burning but was not burnt up.
fact: At the bush God gave his name, «I AM WHO I AM», written in Hebrew as YHWH; Jews show respect by not saying it aloud.
fact: Moses and his brother Aaron asked Pharaoh, «Let my people go», and Pharaoh refused again and again.
fact: Ten plagues struck Egypt, from the river Nile turning to blood to the death of the firstborn.
fact: At the Passover the Israelites marked their doors with lamb's blood, and death passed over their homes; Jews remember it every year at the Passover meal.
fact: Christians link the Passover with the Last Supper, which Jesus ate at Passover time.
fact: God parted the sea so the Israelites crossed on dry ground, and Pharaoh's army was drowned when the water came back.
fact: In the desert God fed the people with manna, bread from heaven, enough for each day; kept overnight it went bad.
fact: The Israelites wandered forty years in the wilderness before reaching the Promised Land, and they often grumbled.
fact: The words «Let my people go» inspired enslaved people in America who sang and fought for their freedom.
fact: Historians disagree about how much of the Exodus story can be traced in Egyptian records; this is disputed.
exercise: Where did Moses' mother hide her baby to save him? | In a basket among the reeds of the Nile | In a cave in the desert | In the house of a Hebrew priest | Under the floor of Pharaoh's palace in the city
exercise: Who found the baby Moses and raised him? | Pharaoh's daughter | Pharaoh's wife | A shepherd's family | His older sister Miriam
exercise: Why are Shiphrah and Puah remembered as heroes? | They refused Pharaoh's order to kill the baby boys | They led the slaves out of Egypt | They hid Moses in the basket | They told Pharaoh about all of the plagues that were coming
exercise: What did the midwives show about orders from powerful people? | It can be right to disobey an order that is unjust | Every order from a king must be obeyed, whatever it is | Only grown men are ever allowed to refuse an order | Breaking any rule is always wrong, whatever the reason
exercise: What was strange about the bush Moses saw? | It burned but was not burnt up | It grew a full metre in a night | It spoke in Pharaoh's voice | It was made of pure gold
exercise: What name did God give Moses at the bush? | I AM WHO I AM | The Lord of Egypt | The King of the Nile | The God of the Desert
exercise: Why do many Jews not say God's name YHWH aloud? | Out of deep respect for God | Because nobody knows its letters | Because it is a secret password | Because Moses forgot it
exercise: What did Moses and Aaron ask Pharaoh again and again? | Let my people go | Give us better houses | Make Moses a prince again | Pay the slaves more money
exercise: How many plagues struck Egypt? | Ten | Seven | Twelve | Forty
exercise: What lesson do Christians draw from Pharaoh's refusals? | Stubborn pride hurts the proud person and many others | Kings always win in the end | Plagues are only bad luck | Saying no is always strong
exercise: What did the Israelites put on their doors at the first Passover? | Lamb's blood | Olive oil | A golden sign | Palm branches
exercise: What do Christians link the Passover with? | The Last Supper of Jesus | The birth of Jesus in Bethlehem | Noah's rainbow after the flood | The building of the Tower of Babel
exercise: What happened at the Red Sea? | God parted the water and the Israelites crossed on dry ground | The Israelites built boats and sailed away | Pharaoh's army let them go in peace | Moses swam across and pulled a rope
exercise: What was manna? | Bread from heaven that fed the people in the desert | A fruit growing on desert trees | Grain the people had carried out with them from Egypt | Fish caught in the Red Sea
exercise: What happened when people kept extra manna overnight? | It went bad, teaching them to trust God for each day | It turned into gold coins | It doubled in size | It fed the whole army of Egypt
exercise: How long did the Israelites wander in the wilderness? | Forty years | Forty days | Seven years | Ten months
exercise: What did the people often do in the wilderness even after being rescued? | They grumbled and complained | They sang happily every single day | They built a great city of stone | They all went straight back to Egypt
exercise: Why did «Let my people go» matter to enslaved people in America? | It showed that God cares about freedom and hates slavery | It was a law passed by their government to set slaves free | It told them to stay quiet and wait | It promised them land in Egypt
test: my cousin says moses was found by pharaoh's wife and she was mean to him. right? | No, Pharaoh's daughter found him and raised him with care | Yes, the wife found him and was cruel | No, his older sister found him and kept him at home herself | Yes, but the wife later set him free
test: «bro the bush was on fire and burnt to ash, then god spoke from the ash» — fix it | The bush burned but was never burnt up, and God spoke from it | The bush was struck by lightning and God shouted from the sky above | The bush was a tree in Pharaoh's garden | The ash formed letters with God's name
test: A Jewish classmate writes «G-d» instead of «God». What is the kindest way to understand this? | It shows respect for God's name, like not saying YHWH aloud | It means she has forgotten how to spell | It means she doesn't believe in God | It is a rude joke about Christians
test: teacher told us to tell on a kid who did nothing wrong or we all get detention. the midwives would say what?? | That an unfair order can be refused, even when it costs you | That you always obey the teacher whatever happens | That the whole class deserves the detention anyway | That you should lie and say you saw nothing
test: Sam says «the plagues were just bad weather, pharaoh let them go after the first one». What is right? | Pharaoh refused again and again, and there were ten plagues | Pharaoh let them go straight after the third plague, the gnats | There were only two plagues and then peace | Pharaoh never let them go at all
test: A friend with no religion asks: «why do Jews have a big dinner every spring about doors?» | Passover remembers how death passed over the marked homes and the people were set free | It is a harvest feast for the new wheat | It remembers the end of the Flood | It celebrates the building of the Temple
test: little bro: «they crossed the red sea on a big bridge moses built» — wrong, so what happened? | God opened the water and they walked across on dry ground | They swam while Moses held a rope | They waited on the shore for many days for the sea to freeze over | They paid sailors to row them over
test: «ugh mum, why can't I hoard the snacks like a normal person» — which part of the Exodus story is about this? | The manna, which went bad when people took more than they needed | The burning bush, which needed no fuel | The ten plagues, which struck Egypt one after another | The basket, where Moses was kept safe
test: kid in class: «I'd never moan if god saved me from slaves lol». What does the wilderness story show? | Even rescued people grumble, so gratitude takes practice | The people never complained once | Only Moses complained in the desert | They were thankful from the very first day right to the very last
test: A judge asks: «Why did freedom songs in America use the words "Let my people go"?» | Enslaved people saw God freeing the Hebrews and hoped he would free them too | The words were the name of a famous ship | The words were written by an American president and sung at all of his parties | The songs were about leaving America for Egypt
test: A judge follows up: «Can we prove from Egyptian records exactly how the Exodus happened?» | No, historians disagree about this, so it is disputed | Yes, every detail is in the Egyptian records | Yes, Pharaoh himself wrote a full diary of it that we can read today | No, because Egypt never existed
exam: Identify the individuals who, pursuant to the account in Exodus, declined to execute the royal decree concerning the Hebrew male infants. | The midwives Shiphrah and Puah | The magicians of Pharaoh's court | The elders of the Israelite tribes | The guards of the royal palace
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the phenomenon from which God first addressed Moses? | A bush that was aflame yet remained unconsumed | A pillar of stone that emitted speech | A river transformed into a sea of fire | A star that descended upon the summit
exam: Select the response consistent with the established account concerning the provision of nourishment in the wilderness. | Manna was supplied daily, and any surplus retained overnight was spoiled | Grain was transported in quantity from the storehouses of Egypt | The Israelites subsisted exclusively upon fish from the Red Sea | Provisions were purchased from merchants in the desert
exam: State the duration of the sojourn of the Israelites in the wilderness prior to their entry into the Promised Land. | Forty years | Forty days | Seven years | Four hundred years
